From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Junod <donujp@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 14:04:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] fix(dht): bound the DHT worker queues and per-lookup
 concurrency

Several DHT queues are unbounded channels, and a lookup can start every
request queued on it, so a slow UDP writer or a slow consumer of
discovered peers lets work and memory pile up without a limit:

 - the datagram queue feeding the socket writer;
 - the per-lookup queues of nodes still to be queried and of peers found;
 - the FuturesUnordered sets that run recursive requests, bucket
   refreshes and pings (all of them poll as many as are queued).

Make them bounded:

 - Outgoing datagrams: 256. A locally initiated request awaits a free
   slot before it allocates a transaction id and registers itself as
   in-flight. Replies and untracked announces are best-effort and are
   dropped when the queue is full, since awaiting in the receive path
   would stop reading the socket.
 - Recursive-node queue: 256, with at most 32 requests running per
   lookup worker. The future set is a small BoundedFuturesUnordered that
   refuses a 33rd push, and the select loops stop receiving while it is
   full. A node dropped because the queue is full is found again by the
   periodic requery of the routing table. A full root queue is reported
   as Saturated (normal requery interval) rather than as empty, which
   would make the worker retry every second.
 - Delivered-peer queue: 256, best-effort (a full queue drops the peer).
 - Bucket refresh and questionable-node ping queues: 256 entries and 32
   concurrent requests per worker.

Bootstrap fan-out is left as it is: its input is the finite configured
list.

Tests check each limit at its exact boundary (accepts N, rejects N+1,
accepts again after one is consumed).
